  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe key idea is to evolve probability distributions while maintaining a feedback loop with the actual overlap objective. This combines the exploration power of probabilistic methods with the precision of gradient-based optimization. We'll start with an initial probability distribution over function forms and parameters, then iteratively refine it by selecting winners in a structured tournament. The innovation lies in maintaining a direct link between the optimization landscape (overlap values) and the probability distribution evolution via a coupling term.\n\nThe rationale is twofold:\n1. Probabilistic Representation provides a flexible way to represent the optimization landscape without committing to a specific function class.\n2. Feedback Mechanism creates a closed-loop between overlap evaluations and distribution updates, unlike feedforward approaches.\n\nThis approach directly targets the underlying problem structure by treating the minimum as a density phenomenon we can explore, rather than using fixed patterns or transformations.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n\nWe consider functions on [0,2] parameterized by \u03b8 = (f_type, f_params) where:\n- f_type \u2208 {1, 2, ..., M} (selected from a set of 8 patterns)\n- f_params \u2208 \u211d^{p_i} for each pattern type\n\nThe function has the form h(x,\u03b8):[0,1]^n\nConstraint: \u222bh dx = 1\n\nObjective to minimize: C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nWe discretize so h \u2208 \u211d^n satisfying:\n    sum(h_i * dx) = 1\n    0 <= h_i <= 1 for all i\n\nRepresentation:\nWe represent solutions as probability vectors \u03c0 \u2208 \u0394^n, and use a mixed-integer programming formulation to build the coupling between parameters and overlap.\n\nFor each candidate function (\u03b8, \u03c6_sparse_at_lag_k) , but global mixing.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\nPhase 1: Initialization\n- Sample n-initial=512 points from uniform distribution over [0,2]\n- For i=0 to n_events-1:\n   - Start a thread of optimization at \u03b8_i (random pattern type)\n   - Use n_step=100 SLSQP evaluations with low precision\n   - Local winner takes the fastest route to feasible area\n\nPhase 2: Tournament Selection\n- Form tournament brackets of 4 progressed candidates per cycle\n- Evaluate C5 for each in the round\n- Select winner via score = -log(C5) * majorityvote_survival\n\nPhase 3: Cross-Training\n- Update global knowledge base with successful atoms\n- Add perturbations based on current iteration\n- Inject noise into parameters with norm <= C_adaptive\n\nPhase 4: Hierarchical Boosting\n- For each surviving pattern type, train a specialized vector quantizer\n- Generate opponent functions via mutation and crossover\n- Apply shadow modeling against detected common pitfalls\n\nKey mathematical structures:\n- Discretized overlap as quadratic form: h^T A h for instability modes\n- Multi-parameter tracking via TD-(n_steps)\n- \u03b1-stability condition on updates\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n1. Pattern Initiators (initial patterns):\n   - Binary oscillating patterns with phase tracking\n   - Wavelet packets at base frequencies\n   - Adaptive windowed sinuses with central tendency\n   - Mutual coherence-minimizing waveforms\n\n2. Optimization Schedules:\n   - Beta schedule: \u03b2_initial=5e0, \u03b2_max=3e3, increasing with success rate\n   - Mutation schedule: \u03c3_start=1e-1, \u03c3_final=3e-1, logarithmic increase\n   - Exploration factor: \u03b7 = 0.3 * (1 - win_ratio) + 0.9\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness:\nTotal: 1300 seconds\nPhase 1: 25%\nPhase 2: 70%\nMin operational time per cycle: 12 seconds if remaining time\n\n- \u0415\u0441\u043b\u0438 \u044d\u043a\u0441\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0438\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0442 >=-3 \u043f\u043e \"\u041b\u0435\u0433\u043a\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0438\", \u0438\u0441\u043f\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0437\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u044c \u0440\u0430\u043d\u043d\u044e\u044e \u0442\u0435\u0440\u043c\u0438\u043d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u044e.\n- Always maintain a shadow model for each pattern type\n- Dust controller (rejection sampling for stuck trials)\n\nDesign checks against pathological cases:\n- Requires validation every 10 iterations\n\nThe method ensures:\n- Global feedback integration via tree search carried in memory\n- Natural adaptation to objective reappraisal\n- Contextual evaluation of each method state\n- Resource priority allocation to promising patterns\n\nMathematical validation provided through:\n- Explicit satisfaction of coupling term in mixed-integer program\n- Weighted rejection via stochastic penalty function\n- Feasibility projection at each iteration\n\nThe strategy eschews dimensional transformations in favor of in-context execution. Implementation must maintain strict record keeping of all parallel workers and shadow processes. The candidate solution ideal is attained through energy minimization over the population space.",
